WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.879
Speaker 1: Last week we had some pretty solid
unemployment numbers or employment numbers.

00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:11.470
This week we received pretty shocking and
unexpected new unemployment numbers.

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:18.520
New weekly jobless claims announced this morning
unexpectedly dropped to their lowest since

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.090
the start of the pandemic.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:27.730
Again, for a second week in a row, we were
expecting 610000 people to file new unemployment

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:28.730
claims.

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:32.210
In the past week, we got five hundred and
forty seven thousand.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:39.050
So we came in more than 10 percent lower than
the expected number of unemployment claims.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.010
That's negative.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:45.370
From the standpoint of positive, I'm sounding
kind of like Trump, what I mean is when you

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:53.470
are measuring something bad, people losing
jobs coming in low is good.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:57.490
I tested very positively from the standpoint
of negative.

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:04.210
OK, for context, during the Great Recession
of the 2000s, the high mark for one week job

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:06.439
losses was six hundred and sixty five thousand.

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:09.360
We are now below that number for two weeks
in a row.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:14.899
And remember that we are 90 percent below
what we were seeing in twenty, twenty, sometimes

00:01:14.899 --> 00:01:18.520
six million new jobless claims in a single
week.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.320
We are about 90 percent below that.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.380
So that's all pointing in the right direction
now.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:23.619
It's not all perfect.

00:01:23.619 --> 00:01:25.280
There's no reason to pretend it is.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:26.399
This is economic for me.

00:01:26.399 --> 00:01:28.030
It's not political.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:31.960
Continuing claims, meaning people who were
already unemployed before this week and are

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.829
still unemployed, that came in slightly higher
than expected at about three point six million

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:37.829
people.

00:01:37.829 --> 00:01:38.829
That's not great.

00:01:38.829 --> 00:01:40.140
We want to keep seeing that go down.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.170
There is still a generally high level of unemployment.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:44.429
Some states it's really high.

00:01:44.429 --> 00:01:46.450
Alaska is an example.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:47.850
Nevada is an example.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:51.530
Now, let's just go through our reminder.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:56.720
The American right wing wants us to think
things are terrible and not improving because

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.859
Joe Biden is now president.

00:01:58.859 --> 00:02:04.130
So they will, as they always do when a Democrat
is in the White House, say that the normal

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:08.399
unemployment rate doesn't tell us the truth,
the truth trademark.

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:11.220
You need to look instead at the you six.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:15.900
The you six includes people who are working
part time because they can't find full time

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:21.930
work, people who are discouraged and they've
stopped looking for work, et cetera.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:22.930
That's fine.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.260
I you know, that's also declining.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:29.069
The you six spiked to more than twenty two
percent at the start of the pandemic.

00:02:29.069 --> 00:02:31.750
It was down to twelve point something when
Trump left.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:33.970
It's now down to ten point something percent.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:38.190
The U.S. is always higher than the U3, but
it's declining as well.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:39.420
And that is a very good thing.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:44.750
You know, people people often want to call
out one measure or another as misleading for

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:48.799
political reasons, as if it's some kind of
malicious conspiracy.

00:02:48.799 --> 00:02:53.120
Each of these indicators is designed to show
something different and to tell us different

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.120
things.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.790
You look at more data points and you have
a deeper understanding of what's going on.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:00.219
No one data point tells you everything.

00:03:00.219 --> 00:03:04.860
Look at the U3, which is the unemployment
rate that's often talked about on the news,

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:10.080
but also look at the you six look at wages,
look at new claims, look at consumer confidence

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.840
and inflation, which we see typically through
the consumer price index.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.330
Look at the stock market.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:20.719
It's also a unique situation right now where
there are people who lost jobs completely

00:03:20.719 --> 00:03:22.530
due to the pandemic.

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:27.890
They're coming back partially now as businesses
start to reopen, many of those people who

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:31.280
are currently back part time, but they used
to be full time.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:33.730
They will go back to being full time eventually.

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:35.890
Not all, but many will.

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:42.110
There are some areas and industries that actually
need more people now than they did before

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:48.420
the pandemic, because there are some industries
on hiring sprees trying to resolve the supply

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.340
chain issues that were exposed by the pandemic.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:58.909
Remember that in March, April, May of twenty
twenty, there are really examples or anecdotes

00:03:58.909 --> 00:04:01.299
of everything for people who want to find
them.

00:04:01.299 --> 00:04:03.709
But the big picture is that things are improving.

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:09.750
Not surprisingly, and as we said before, the
biggest indicators of how things will go in

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.590
the coming weeks are not economic numbers.

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:14.109
They are medical numbers.

00:04:14.109 --> 00:04:21.490
When cases trend down and deaths trend down,
the economic indicators are likely and and

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:24.590
reasonably expected to improve.

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:28.480
Massachusetts is now down to just ten covid
deaths a day.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:31.600
That is the lowest number since March of twenty
twenty.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:33.020
Understand the scope of that.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:39.710
Massachusetts now has reduced daily covid
deaths to a number we haven't seen since faught

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:42.260
thirteen, fourteen months ago.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:47.030
That is a really good economic indicator,
even though it's not an economic indicator,

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.410
it's actually a medical indicator.

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:50.890
So we know what we have to do.

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.150
We see the numbers starting to improve.

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:57.090
Now it's a question of are people going to
get vaccinated?

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:58.090
It would not be surprising.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:05.830
I was reading a piece from Doctor Dr. Ashish
Shah, or I believe his name is from Brown

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:07.180
University yesterday.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:09.460
He is saying.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:16.650
Contagion outdoors is so low, we believe maybe
one in a thousand cases are transmitted outdoors

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:21.310
that things are looking good enough that we
could probably do away with any outdoor masking

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:24.490
mandate states that still have them relatively
soon.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:29.660
These these bits of good news are going to
accelerate the more quickly that cases and

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:33.690
deaths go down, which is, of course, driven
by vaccinations going up.

